## Decision for this review

Keep the parser and the booking-form hint as separate problems, each with its own engineering
ticket. The export's incident narrative says "one issue, one fix queued", but the running
classification and tenant directory show two causes. A `known` label describes the desk's
classification; it is not evidence that every report has the same cause, or that a fix shipped.

<Callout kind="warn">
This is a review of the 4 October 2026 export, not a live view of the support desk. No fix is
verified here. The original incident page and the accepted 2024 decision are historical evidence,
not the current rule recommended by this review.
</Callout>

## Computed scope and tickets

<ReviewEvidence />

The counts use one report per ID, filter `mergedInto === 4471`, and call the supplied
`groupByCause` / `causeOf` logic with `tenantOf`. The tenant's locale and deployed booking form
decide the cause. `meta.browserLocale` and `meta.intakeTag` are intake observations, not the rule.
The two sets are disjoint and account for every unique report in the ticket.

### Parser: month-first interpretation of day-first dates

[Parser ticket](https://github.com/rkondratowicz/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1).
The shared parser treats date text as month/day for tenants whose locale puts the day first.
For example, `R-41203` reports 2 December becoming 12 February. `R-41205` reports 3 October
becoming 10 March despite intake tagging it `hint` from an en-US browser.

Engineering should use an explicit date contract at the parsing boundary, validate impossible
dates, and test day-first and month-first input plus app, email and form paths. Do not silently
change the form hint and call this fixed: it is a separate path.

### Hint: booking-v3 prints the wrong date order

[Hint ticket](https://github.com/rkondratowicz/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2).
For a day-first tenant, booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY`, but the form reads the entered date in
the tenant's day-first order. A customer who follows that hint therefore gets the wrong date.
The affected form tenants are Alder Physio, Copper Studio and Pale Harbour. Their app and
email reports still follow the parser rule; tenant name alone is not enough.

For example, `R-41241` is an Alder Physio form report: 6 August became 8 June. Correct the hint
to match the form's accepted locale order, with tests for booking-v2 and booking-v3. Verify both
the displayed hint and the date submitted. A shared-parser fix alone does not establish that
this misleading instruction is repaired.

## What remains uncertain

The desk flagged `R-41202`, `R-41209`, `R-41236`, `R-41344` and `R-41620` as one-day moves.
Their bodies include a one-day shift, an invite arriving before booking, and different days in
Lisbon and Toronto. The supplied rule still files all of them under parser. Keep them there for
this reconciliation, but retain the IDs and request original timestamps, time zones and raw
inputs before diagnosing a third bug. Do not invent a timezone fix from the wording.

## The queue kept moving

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Reports arrived in April, May and June before the first merges on 2 July. A July merge spike is
partly backlog processing, not a July onset. Receipt counts continue into October; marking the
ticket known did not stop reports. Neither series proves when a fix deployed, how many users
were affected, or whether the incident is over. Inspect new arrivals against their tenants and
form versions before moving them; rerun the counts when the export changes.

## Triage by cause, not by subject

The [accepted 2024 decision](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject/) and
[old triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe/) are well beyond the KB's 180-day review threshold.
Their confident wording is not a substitute for checking the rendered examples:

<DedupeExplorer />

Different subjects can share a cause. Identical subjects can have different causes because
the tenant or channel differs. An unknown tenant provides no basis for merging, even when
the subjects match. Missing evidence should stay unplaced for investigation.

See [decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause/) for the replacement rule proposed for
the desk. It preserves decision 0001 unchanged and records why this review rejects subject-only
merging. The desk must approve operational adoption; this repo does not change production.

